    type: "theory",
    duration: "25 min",
    content: `HTML — HyperText Markup Language — is the foundational language of the World Wide Web. Every webpage you have ever visited, from Google's search results to a local Cameroonian news portal, is ultimately built on HTML. Understanding HTML is the first and most essential step in becoming a web developer.\n\nHTML was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1991 while working at CERN, the European physics research organization. His original goal was simple: create a way for scientists to share documents over a network. He combined the concept of hypertext (text that links to other text) with a markup language (a system for annotating documents). The result was HTML — a format that tells the browser both what content to display and how to structure it.\n\nAt its core, HTML is a markup language, not a programming language. This is an important distinction. A programming language like JavaScript can perform calculations, make decisions, and control logic flow. HTML simply describes the structure and meaning of content. You use HTML to say: "this text is a heading," "this is a paragraph," "this is a list," or "this is an image." The browser then reads those descriptions and renders them visually.\n\nHTML documents are plain text files with a .html extension. They consist of a series of elements, each defined by tags. A tag is a keyword enclosed in angle brackets. Most elements have an opening tag and a closing tag:\n<h1>My First Heading</h1>\nHere <h1> is the opening tag, "My First Heading" is the content, and </h1> is the closing tag. Together, they form an HTML element. The browser reads this and renders the text as a large heading.\n\nSome elements are self-closing, meaning they do not wrap content and have no closing tag. The <img> tag for images and the <br> tag for line breaks are examples:\n<img src="photo.jpg" alt="A photograph">\n<br>\n\nAttributes provide additional information about elements. They appear inside the opening tag as name="value" pairs. In the image example above, src specifies the image file path and alt provides alternative text for screen readers and when the image fails to load.\n\nThe history of HTML versions shows continuous evolution. HTML 4 (1997) standardized the web. XHTML (2000) enforced stricter syntax. HTML5 (2014) is the current standard, introducing semantic elements, native audio/video support, canvas drawing, and web storage APIs — features that define the modern web.\n\nWhen you open a browser like Chrome or Firefox and navigate to a URL, the browser sends an HTTP request to a server, which responds with an HTML file. The browser's rendering engine (Blink in Chrome, Gecko in Firefox) parses that HTML, builds a Document Object Model (DOM) — a tree-like representation of the page — and paints the result on screen. This entire process happens in milliseconds.\n\nFor a student in Cameroon learning web development, HTML is your starting point. Every web application you will ever build — whether it is a job portal for Douala businesses, an e-learning platform, or a government services website — begins with a solid HTML foundation. Master HTML first, and JavaScript and CSS will feel logical extensions of it.`,
