    type: "theory",
    duration: "25 min",
    content: `Every valid HTML5 document follows a standard structural skeleton. Understanding this skeleton is essential because it tells browsers how to interpret your page, instructs search engines how to index it, and communicates the document's language and encoding to user agents worldwide.\n\nThe very first line of any modern HTML document must be the doctype declaration:\n<!DOCTYPE html>\nThis is not an HTML tag — it is an instruction to the browser. It tells the browser to render the page using the modern HTML5 standard rather than legacy quirks mode. In older browsers, omitting this declaration causes unpredictable rendering behaviour. Always include it as the absolute first line.\n\nNext comes the <html> element, which is the root element of the entire document. Every other element is a descendant of this root. The lang attribute is critically important here:\n<html lang="en">\nThis attribute declares the primary language of the document. Screen readers use it to select the correct pronunciation engine. Search engines use it for language-specific indexing. For a French-language Cameroonian website, you would write lang="fr".\n\nInside <html>, the document is divided into two major sections: <head> and <body>.\n\nThe <head> element contains metadata — information about the document that is not displayed directly to the user but is essential for browsers and search engines. Key elements inside <head> include:\n\n<meta charset="UTF-8"> — Declares the character encoding. UTF-8 supports virtually all characters in all human languages, including French accented characters (é, è, ç) commonly used in Cameroonian French-language websites.\n\n<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"> — The viewport meta tag is essential for responsive design. Without it, mobile browsers render the page at full desktop width and then zoom out, making text unreadably small. This tag instructs the browser to match the device's width and set zoom to 1.\n\n<title>Page Title Here</title> — Sets the document title shown in browser tabs and used by search engines as the primary heading in search results. Every page must have a unique, descriptive title.\n\n<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"> — Links external CSS files to the document.\n\n<script src="app.js" defer></script> — Links JavaScript files. The defer attribute ensures the script executes after the HTML is fully parsed, preventing errors from referencing elements that haven't loaded yet.\n\nThe <body> element contains all visible content of the page — everything the user sees and interacts with: text, images, buttons, forms, navigation menus, and more.\n\nA complete minimal HTML5 document:\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang="en">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset="UTF-8">\n    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">\n    <title>My Web Page</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Welcome to my website</h1>\n    <p>This is a paragraph of text.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\nProper indentation (using 2 or 4 spaces per level) is a professional discipline. While browsers ignore whitespace, well-indented HTML is significantly easier to read, debug, and maintain — especially in team projects where multiple developers work on the same codebase.`,
