    title: "CSS Selectors & Specificity",
    type: "theory",
    duration: "30 min",
    content: `CSS selectors are patterns used to target specific HTML elements for styling. Mastering selectors and understanding specificity is essential for writing predictable, maintainable CSS without constantly fighting your own stylesheet with !important overrides.\n\nBasic Selectors:\n\nElement selector — targets all elements of that type:\np { color: #333; } /* all paragraphs */\n\nClass selector — targets elements with a specific class (prefix with dot):\n.card { background: white; padding: 1rem; } /* all elements with class="card" */\n\nID selector — targets the single element with a specific ID (prefix with #):\n#hero-section { min-height: 100vh; } /* the element with id="hero-section" */\n\nAttribute selector — targets elements with specific attributes:\ninput[type="email"] { border: 1px solid blue; }\na[href^="https"] { color: green; } /* links starting with https */\na[href$=".pdf"] { color: red; } /* links ending in .pdf */\n\nPseudo-class selectors — target elements in a specific state:\na:hover { text-decoration: underline; }\ninput:focus { outline: 2px solid #362C28; }\nli:first-child { font-weight: bold; }\nli:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }\nli:nth-child(2n) { background: #f5f5f5; } /* even items */\n:not(.active) { opacity: 0.6; } /* elements without class 'active' }\n\nPseudo-element selectors — style a specific part of an element:\np::first-line { font-weight: bold; }\np::first-letter { font-size: 2em; float: left; }\n::before { content: "»"; } /* Insert content before element */\n::after { content: "«"; } /* Insert content after element */\n::placeholder { color: #999; } /* Input placeholder text */\n\nCombinators — express relationships between elements:\ndiv p { color: blue; } /* Descendant: any p inside any div */\ndiv > p { color: blue; } /* Direct child: p directly inside div */\nh2 + p { margin-top: 0; } /* Adjacent sibling: p immediately after h2 */\nh2 ~ p { color: gray; } /* General sibling: any p after h2 in same parent */\n\nGrouping Selectors — apply the same styles to multiple selectors:\nh1, h2, h3, h4 { font-family: Georgia, serif; }\n\nSpecificity is the algorithm that determines which CSS rule wins when multiple rules target the same element. Specificity is calculated as a three-number score (A, B, C):\n- A: Number of ID selectors (#id)\n- B: Number of class selectors (.class), attribute selectors ([attr]), and pseudo-classes (:hover)\n- C: Number of element selectors (p, div) and pseudo-elements (::before)\n\nExamples:\n- p → (0, 0, 1)\n- .card → (0, 1, 0)\n- #hero → (1, 0, 0)\n- #hero .card p → (1, 1, 1)\n- div.card p.text → (0, 2, 2)\n\nA higher specificity score always wins, regardless of rule order. ID selectors always beat class selectors, which always beat element selectors.\n\nThe !important declaration overrides all specificity calculations:\np { color: red !important; }\n\nHowever, !important should be avoided in normal code — it creates a specificity arms race where all future developers must add !important to override it, resulting in increasingly fragile CSS. Acceptable use cases: utility classes, third-party overrides, accessibility requirements.\n\nBest practice for specificity management: Keep specificity low and consistent. Prefer class selectors over ID selectors for styling. Structure your CSS with a naming methodology like BEM (Block Element Modifier) to avoid specificity conflicts.`,
